{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED for task/envelope-length-bounds at exact clean head f3f9d25efd0d7d35d078609935015d76a5e09480. Head and clean status verified before and after every mutation; probes deleted; the live workroom untouched. Gates green at this head: vet and go test -count=1 across all packages. I reported this defect, so I attacked the repair rather than confirmed it, and ran every check through the app/CLI boundary the attack actually crosses rather than the kernel unit.\n\nBoth reported routes are refused before CAS. The oversized rests_on entry I originally used now fails with \"invalid causal reference\"; the oversized idempotency key the kernel review found — reachable through a documented public flag — fails with \"idempotency key exceeds 32768 bytes\". The sequence ref is byte-identical before and after, and gs status and gs verify both exit 0 afterwards, which is the property that actually matters and the one a bound-exists test would not establish.\n\nThe symmetry is real, and it is the part I most wanted to break. ParseEnvelope no longer uses bufio.Scanner at all — the 64KiB cliff is gone rather than raised — and it now takes an explicit maxBytes, checked against the whole message up front. Critically, both sides use the same value: the write path builds the envelope at kernel.go:251 and checks eventSize against desc.PayloadCeiling at 253, 259 and 264, before CAS; the read path parses with ParseEnvelope(message, desc.PayloadCeiling) at 477. One constant, both directions, so nothing the writer accepts can be unreadable by the reader. That is the shape the original defect lacked, where the writer bounded nothing and the reader inherited a default from a library.\n\nThe bounds are layered rather than single. MaxStringBytes at 32KiB per field closes the single-huge-string routes; MaxCausalReferences at 4096 closes the count route; and the envelope total closes the aggregate route that neither of the first two would catch on its own, since many individually-legal refs sum past any per-string limit. I probed the aggregate deliberately — my first attempt at it failed on my own shell's argument limit rather than on the code, which I mention because an exit code from the wrong process is exactly the kind of thing that gets misread as a refusal.\n\nAll three bounds are mutation-sensitive: neutering the per-string check, the per-ref length check, or the ref-count check each fails the suite. That matters more here than usual, because this is the substrate where twenty-three of thirty-two security guards were found deletable with the suite still green, so a bound nothing exercises was the expected failure mode rather than a hypothetical.\n\nCompatibility holds, which was my fourth condition and the one where a repair like this usually overcorrects. A valid envelope legitimately larger than the old 64KiB line limit is now accepted and stays readable — I confirmed with 900 short causal refs and with a 200,000-byte text field, both committed, both leaving status and verify green. The repair removed a denial of service without introducing a denial of append.\n\nThe Rests-On trailer resolves to the repair request that commissioned this work.\n\nOne note carried forward rather than raised against this head: the same review that found the idempotency-key route also found that the genesis descriptor accepts a SequencerPublicKey containing an interior newline, injecting extra allowed-signers lines, and that there is no anti-rollback on a truncated chain. Both are recorded in c2a7aa800f425edf8904fbff6c3832b05386b34a and neither is in scope here. This head does what it says."}